  << java.lang.Thread as a descriptor over the SLeeLa sleep builtin: name,
     priority, daemon flag, id, interrupt flag, the Runnable target and run(),
     plus the static sleep. start() keeps its exact-signature stub, because the
     spawn builtin takes a method symbol of the current class and cannot carry
     a Runnable target; a thread that was never started is not alive, and
     join on it returns at once, as Java does. The compiler has no subtype
     assignment yet, so a user class that implements Runnable cannot be passed
     to the Runnable constructors; run() calls the target once that works. >>
